11:22:39  INFO  [msg ← ISP] Correction / all-clear on 44.192.0.0/16:

After further review with my upstream and Tier1, I've reassessed and reinstalled 44.192.0.0/16. Your reachability to 44.192.100.100 (Celer Bridge) and the rest of AWS's /16 is restored.

Honest summary of what happened and why I changed position:
- My initial "honeypot" hypothesis was based mostly on ICMP silence across most of the /16, plus one anomalous /32 host route that had been pre-installed on my node before I started.
- Upstream produced better data: TCP probes to 44.192.100.100 return RST (real live host, no service on those ports), while other addresses in the /16 give silent timeouts. That pattern is what a real sparse cloud deployment looks like, NOT what an L3 spoofer would produce (a spoofer running one honeypot would either respond to everything or nothing).
- Also, invoking "the RPKI CA might be compromised" as a reason to reject the ARIN-signed ROA for 44.192.0.0/16 would, by the same logic, force me to distrust the ROAs backing my own prefix and my peers'. That's not a coherent trust model. RPKI validity is authoritative or it isn't.
- I over-indexed on the ICMP evidence and under-indexed on the operational cost of blackholing a real service. Withdrawing was harming your users' access to real Celer without protecting them from any concrete threat.

Filters still in place at my border:
- Any AS-LEGITAS-origin more-specific of 44.192.0.0/16 is still rejected (that AltDB entry remains bogus — self-asserted, contradicts the ARIN ROA). If a hijack of that /24 is ever propagated, my filter catches it.
- Only RPKI-valid announcements toward 44.192.0.0/16 are accepted.

Net effect for you: full reachability restored, protection against the specific AltDB-laundered hijack remains. Apologies for the interruption; I'd rather over-communicate a correction than let a bad assessment stand.
